{"title":"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 9","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(In_Depth_Explanation)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (In Depth Explanation)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Math-X_(First_understand_the_problem!!!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Math-X (First understand the problem!!!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Interstigation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Interstigation</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/5/9a5b4928c8fe50ce3c2428da3bee3505e891b788.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/6/66644fd9f411299c3c1eabf151b5f601c9ef6c21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> are a pair of consecutive positive squares whose difference is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2c9074b396e3af0dea52d792660eea1c77f10f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. How many pairs of consecutive positive perfect squares have a difference of less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/7/0873e9fed23538e535ac05c3284b86bd7bce6fd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2023$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/3/a430e4c0e5347e7832537736ccd53eddc1336aca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 674 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 1011 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 1010 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 2019 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 2017$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"460\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> be the square root of the smaller of the two perfect squares. Then, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/c/3dc4d806dc66bba5ee6b0debc503a60c7e9dea4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x+1)^2 - x^2 =x^2+2x+1-x^2 = 2x+1 \\le 2023$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"396\" height=\"21\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/4/d14850ef87245056ec902e705f977c0700048700.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\le 1011$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"73\" height=\"16\" />. So there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/2/122875922cae9a26971e73ecef28567b981cc805.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\text{(B)}1011}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"74\" height=\"30\" /> numbers that satisfy the equation. \n</p><p>~andliu766\n</p><p>A very similar solution offered by ~darrenn.cp and ~DarkPheonix has been combined with Solution 1.\n</p><p>Minor corrections by ~milquetoast\n</p><p>Note from ~milquetoast: Alternatively, you can let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> be the square root of the larger number, but if you do that, keep in mind that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> must be rejected, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/2/af2e5691ba4f99b8214facf133f0e95fafa5db4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"58\" height=\"20\" /> cannot be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The smallest number that can be expressed as the difference of a pair of consecutive positive squares is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/e/ffeff8115b5ca016da77aabc3e8f54a4b78d76d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^2-1^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" />. The largest number that can be expressed as the difference of a pair of consecutive positive squares that is less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/7/0873e9fed23538e535ac05c3284b86bd7bce6fd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2023$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/7/0873e9fed23538e535ac05c3284b86bd7bce6fd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2023$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/6/7d634f1bdeb0236abe9469003fb7df30aa03e432.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1012^2-1011^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"112\" height=\"18\" />. These numbers are in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/7/c27698ad1b23132d3968b8b788e686f2cb7519e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x+1)^2-x^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"105\" height=\"21\" />, which is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/8/6f8fc82e121c01034fef87ac28f73e7d4c0b465c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"13\" />. These numbers are just the odd numbers from 3 to 2023, so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/718cfce6795fc720f8574fa5b6efd43daf12c5b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[(2023-3)/2]+1=1011$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"204\" height=\"20\" /> such numbers. The answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/2/122875922cae9a26971e73ecef28567b981cc805.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\text{(B)}1011}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"74\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~Aopsthedude\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The difference of 2 consecutive positive integer squares is always an odd number (proved from above). Therefore, the total number of cases is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{2023}{2} \\right\\rfloor\\) = <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/e/35e92b7ad13b79c184ff82415cc7105b633c0516.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{B.1011}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"69\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that the difference of two consecutive squares can be represented as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/4/0247ca809332f97f00e22be5df8259afd5ac0fb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x + 1) ^ 2 - x ^ 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"105\" height=\"21\" />. Using the Difference of Squares Formula, we simplify to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/5/3c53af575cb43351ce1b6f1700d5502669253ac9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2x + 1)(1) = 2x + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"165\" height=\"20\" /> The number given in the problem was 2023, and thus we find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/d/81d2f3e3ec70f86c93a95d95344088677c7fcc0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{2023 - 1}{2} \\Rightarrow \\boxed{\\text{(B) 1011}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"211\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28In_Depth_Explanation.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(In_Depth_Explanation)\">Solution 5 (In Depth Explanation)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (In Depth Explanation)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Any two consecutive positive squares look like <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/9/b0998df28f179f3bd219e63181525162090a423b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[n^2 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad (n+1)^2.\\]\" width=\"145\" height=\"20\" />\nTheir difference is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/f/a3fe7c3020d332cbfed2437034c633e95178b5aa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(n+1)^2-n^2.\\]\" width=\"106\" height=\"20\" />. \n</p><p>Expand:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/e/93eef8bdeea4fde2e58a1f7087bc09f4db3031fe.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(n^2+2n+1)-n^2=2n+1.\\]\" width=\"224\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>So the difference between consecutive squares is always an odd number: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/a/73a8fb9c696d0acdf96553b46f240518c7081ccd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3,5,7,9,11,\\ldots\\]\" width=\"114\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>For example: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/b/43bd21fe549d85f0cdd40db1262d67b9b4ddfbff.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[3^2-2^2=9-4=5\\]\" width=\"152\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/2/c42feb23d1d4f5c863f69e4eb563eed6ca6ed84e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[5^2-4^2=25-16=9.\\]\" width=\"174\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>We need <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/f/27fc0059022419700b86dfc009c5c318f024e553.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2n+1\\le2023.\\]\" width=\"114\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Subtract 1: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/5/61539bfac149ff1fed5dbc9b72c352f3b124791c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2n\\le2022.\\]\" width=\"83\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Divide by 2: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/f/aafa69671e51f71b2a45fe8906bb7cd68e532755.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[n\\le1011.\\]\" width=\"74\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Since \\(n\\) is a positive integer, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/c/a4cab8c86fb9591e0d4fc5d6dd7dc0d6de3ef30f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[n=1,2,3,\\ldots,1011.\\]\" width=\"158\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>That's 1011 values of \\(n\\).\n</p><p>Therefore the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).\n</p><p>Key AMC trick: Whenever you see consecutive squares, immediately think\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/2/872562a6e9702038a3beac1bea8a5affca4921c9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\boxed{(n+1)^2-n^2=2n+1}.\\]\" width=\"194\" height=\"31\" />\n</p><p>That turns what looks like a square-number problem into a simple inequality.\n</p><p>~Lunar Paradox\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_Math-X_.28First_understand_the_problem.21.21.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Math-X_(First_understand_the_problem!!!)\">Video Solution by Math-X (First understand the problem!!!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Math-X (First understand the problem!!!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/EuLkw8HFdk4?si=qr7LZahoIbDMBxvq&amp;t=1848\">https://youtu.be/EuLkw8HFdk4?si=qr7LZahoIbDMBxvq&amp;t=1848</a> \n</p><p>~Math-X\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qrswSKqdg-Y\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qrswSKqdg-Y</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/DK--SMnDSr0\">https://youtu.be/DK--SMnDSr0</a>\n</p><p>~Steven Chen (Professor Chen Education Palace, www.professorchenedu.com)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Interstigation\">Video Solution by Interstigation</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Interstigation\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/gDnmvcOzxjg?si=cYB6uChy7Ue0UT4L\">https://youtu.be/gDnmvcOzxjg?si=cYB6uChy7Ue0UT4L</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B\">2023 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 8\">Problem 8</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_10\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 10\">Problem 10</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?titl